From John.Gavin at ubsw.com  Thu Jun  1 17:27:22 2000
From: John.Gavin at ubsw.com
Date: Thu, 01 Jun 2000 18:27:22 +0100
Subject: [S] Summary - How can I fit a t-distribution to a sample?
Message-ID: <H00004ac0b7eea3d@MHS>

Hi, 

I recently asked 

> How can I fit a t-distribution to a sample?

There were several replies most of which
appeared on the list, so I will only summarise here.

Overall the problem was not as straighforward
as I expected, however the revised code below 
is a big improvement on my previous attempt.

Matt Calder suggested fitting one parameter at a time
conditional on the current best-guess for the other two. 
Then iterate to convergence.

Bill Venables corrected my likelihood function,
and highlighted the possibility of multiple
stationary points even in the 1-dimensional case.
Here also warned that there is little shape difference 
in the t family above about 8 degrees of freedom (df), 
so larger samples might be required and the 
standard error for this parameter will be large.
All of these problems were encountered in tests.

I wasnt able to incorporate partial derivatives.

Albyn Jones suggested a smooth transformation 
of the df parameter from (0, Inf] to (0, 1].
This didnt produce a noticable imporvement
for the limited number of cases I ran.

Andreas Christmann offered several references
which I will try to follow up on.

Noone mentioned fitting by method of moments
so I presume that this is not a realistic route
(some of the moments require evaluation of a Bessel
function but I am not aware of SPlus code for this).

Thanks to Andreas Christmann, Bill Venables, 
Albyn Jones and Matt Calder.
Their full set of replies is available on request.

Regards,

John.

John Gavin
Quantitative Risk: Models and Statistics
UBS Warburg, 
1st floor, 1 Finsbury Ave., London EC2M 2PP.
john.gavin at ubsw.com
+44 (0) 20 7567 4289

tfit   <- function(x, digits = 2 , maxIter = 30 , trace = F)
# Purpose  Estimates scale, location and degrees of freedom
#          for a sample from a t-distribution.
# Example  set.seed(21)
#          ans <- tfit(x = rt(100, 5) * 5 + 5, trace = T )
#          ans$params ; ans$stepNum ; ans$converged
# x        Data sample.
#          This is meant to be from a t-distribution
#          with varying location (loc), scale (scale)
#          and 'fatness' (df) parameters.,
# digits   number of decimal digits after decimal point that
#          must agree else iterate again.
#          Note - this is the same for all three parameters. 
# maxIter  max number of iterations before aborting.
# trace    Summary info for each iteration.
# Output   Output is a list, including estimated parameters (params)
# Notes    Convergence may suffer from local minima,
#          especially if df > 8 and scale > 10. 
#          In such cases, try increasing the sample size.
#          Optimisation is over a arbitrary intervals that are hard coded,
#          e.g. 0 to 100 for the scale parameter.
{  # guess the starting values
   loc0 <- median(x) ; scale0 <- mad(x) ; 
   # reparametrize by mapping df by a smooth transformation 
   # onto the interval [0,1], where 1 corresponds to infinite df.  
   # alpha = df/(c + df), the constant c = df corresponding to alpha = .5. 
   assign("alpha2df", function(x) (6 * x) / (1 - x) , frame = 1)
   alpha     <- .9 # arbitrarily start at about df = 50
   df0       <- alpha2df(alpha)
   if (trace) print(round(c(loc = loc0, scale = scale0, df = df0), 5) )
   tfitloc   <- function(loc, x, scale, df) 
      sum(log( dt( ( x - loc ) / scale , df) ) - log(scale) )
   tfitscale <- function(scale, x, loc, df) 
      sum(log( dt( ( x - loc ) / scale , df) ) - log(scale) )
   tfitalpha <- function(alpha, x, loc, scale) 
      sum(log( dt( ( x - loc ) / scale , alpha2df(alpha) ) ) - log(scale) )
   # fit one parameter at a time and iterate to convergence
   iter <- 0 ; change <- 1 ; 
   while ( change > 0 && (iter <- iter + 1) <= maxIter )
   {  loc1   <- optimize(tfitloc,   c(-1000, 1000), x = x , scale = scale0, 
         df    = df0,    max = T)$maximum
      scale1 <- optimize(tfitscale, c(0, 100),      x = x , loc   = loc1,   
         df    = df0,    max = T)$maximum
      alpha  <- optimize(tfitalpha, c(0, 1),        x = x , loc   = loc1, 
         scale = scale1, max = T)$maximum
      df1    <- alpha2df(alpha)
      change <- round(10^digits * max(abs(loc0 - loc1), abs(scale0 - scale1), 
         abs(df0 - df1) ) , 0)
      loc0   <- loc1 ; scale0 <- scale1 ; df0 <- df1
      if (trace)
      {   ans      <- round(c(step = iter, loc = loc0, scale = scale0, 
             df = df0, change = change), digits + 2) 
         cat( paste( names(ans), ans, sep = "=", collapse = " " ), "\n")
      }  # if
   }  # while
   list( params = c(location = loc0, scale = scale0, df = df0),
      stepNum = iter, converged = iter <= maxIter, data = x)
}

set.seed(21)
ans <- tfit(x = rt(100, 5) * 5 + 5, trace = T )
ans$params ; ans$stepNum ; ans$converged

