Most important, Thanks to all. (If I missed a reply, my apology)
(The usual warnings apply, I haven't tested this code)

_________________________________________________________________________
I. From Franz Mueter

apply(Data,2,function(x) sum(!is.na(x))<=2)

will return a logical vector that is T for each variable in Data that has 2
or fewer non-missing observations
_________________________________________________________________________
IIA. Two verssions from Chuck Cleland

On second reading, it looks like you want to exclude to columns that don't
have at
least two non-missing values.

mydata[ , apply(is.na(mydata), 2, function(x){sum(x==F)}) >= 2]
_________________________________________________________________________
IIB. Two verssions from Chuck Cleland
To count the number of missing values in each column

    apply(is.na(mydata), 2, function(x){sum(as.numeric(x))})

To select the columns that have less than 2 missing values

    mydata[,apply(is.na(mydata), 2, function(x){sum(as.numeric(x))}) <= 2]

_________________________________________________________________________
IV Nick Locantore

        Suppose your data frame is called rowtest.  apply should do the
trick.  Just make the function count the non-missings in the row.  My
dataframe test was 20x5.  There is most likely a quicker ways to do this.

# test is a 20x5 data frame with some missings interspersed
rowtest <- apply(test, 1, function(x){sum(!is.na(x))})
rows <- 1:20
out <- rows[rowtest < 3]
# out should be the variables with 2 or fewer non-missings

Nick Locantore
_________________________________________________________________________

V. Tom Richards

names(mth1srv)[sapply(names(mth1srv),function(x){sum(!is.na(mth1srv[,x]))<=2
})]
_________________________________________________________________________

VI. Bill Dunlap <Hi Bill!! <grin>>
sapply involves a loop but will probably be quick enough:

   num.not.missing <- sapply(dataframe, function(column)sum(!is.na(column)))

_________________________________________________________________________

VII. Rolf Turner

(1:ncol(x))[unlist(lapply(x,function(y){sum(!is.na(y)) <= 2}))]

will give you the column numbers of such variables.

(names(x))[unlist(lapply(x,function(y){sum(!is.na(y)) <= 2}))]

will give you their names.

(I think!  I just keyed in the above code without any testing whatever.)

