Subject: [S] Removing outliers with help from loess
Message-ID: <C71CB7425639D311A7A40008C7286AB001984B4D@carina.phibred.com>

I have a plot of data (x,y) for which the expectation function of any
underlying model is most decidedly nonlinear.  I'm trying to develop a
procedure for removing outliers from the data with the help of loess.

My idea was to compute the std err of the loess fit and then construct
confidence bands for the distribution of the DATA (not the loess curve)
which could be used for finding possible outliers.  Alas, I'm not sure how
or if it is possible to go from the std error of the location for the loess
curve to a std dev for the distribution of the data at each value of x.  I
doubt if it is so simple as to scale up by sqrt(n) where n is the sample
size at a given value of x, i.e. stddev=stderr*sqrt(n).  I've done some
searching, but have not found any examples or theory related to my ideas.

Are there any references related to my proposed idea, or does anybody have
comments on this?  Even "I'm not sure if there's any theory to support this"
(a la Brian Ripley) would be useful.

I'll summarize any helpful emails.

Kevin

For the curious...

>loessfit_loess(y~x,data=S.dat)
>conf_pointwise(predict(loessfit,newdata=0:120,se.fit=T),coverage=0.90)

