Subject: [S] estimating the upper limit of the support for a truncated exponential r.v.
Message-ID: <NDBBKPBLOLKNDPPMIFMOIEFACEAA.stevec@berl.ab.ca>

Splus-ers, this is really a statistics question: 
I have a manuscript under review where I characterise
a phenomena (the  log of the size of forest fires) as
a truncated exponential random variable.

I estimate the upper limit of the support from my
data by least squares, and bootstrap an estimate
of the parameter variance-covariance matrix.
I did this because the only other estimation
method I know (maximum likelihood) does not work
in this case: it necessarily converges on the
sample maximum, which is not reasonable. 

One of my reviewers challenges this approach: can
anyone suggest either a better estimator, or
a reference justifying my approach? I can't find
anything in the literature I know.

Any comments or pointers would be most helpful and
welcome.

Cheers

Steve Cumming

