From: Max Kuhn@BDX on 06/02/2000 02:26 PM

I agree with Rolf, although less emphatically. I don't want to get of the topic
but....

I think that this comes under the heading of "which software do I use for
analysis X?" I think that most would agree that different packages have
different strengths and weaknesses. Where would I analyze a response surface
model? I could do it in SAS (Proc RSreg), S-Plus (the Dox module), Minitab, Jmp
or Design Expert to name a few. I would end up doing the analysis in one of the
last three because they are better suited for these types of analyses (sorry
Perry if you're reading this). I think it's easy to conceive of things that are
implemented better in S-plus than SAS (such as anything graphical). Is the
converse not true? Of course, if you only have S-Plus then this is bad news, but
I wouldn't be too hard on S-Plus.

Incidentally, I think that "modern" mixed models (using lme here or Proc Mixed
in SAS) are very useful for designed experiments, especially split plots, for a
few reasons. First, they are flexible enough they deal with non-textbook data
well. In real life, there always seems to be a replicate that cannot be tested
or used. Also, other issues arise such as spatial correlations in the plots
which can be handled well. It's not hard to define multiple random effects with
different "subject" indicators and so on. Secondly, why should I deal with
expected mean squares? Assuming that I do a good job specifying what is fixed or
random, an analysis using something like lme will give me estimates of the
variance components and the fixed effects without any mean squares (or other
'blithering confusions'). I know that the determination of EMS is transparent in
the software, it just seems more natrual to me to figure out the fixed and
random effects and directly estimate the parameters.

On that note, if you have nlme3, it comes with some split plot examples. See the
Semicond, Cultivar and CultMiss files in the SAS_mxd directory for some
examples.

Max

To:   s-news at wubios.wustl.edu
cc:    (bcc: Max Kuhn/BALT/BDX)
Subject:  [S] Model syntax for split plot designs

Kent E. Holsinger writes:

>  I'm having trouble with what I'm sure is a very simple problem, i.e.,
>  specifying the correct syntax for a simple split plot design. To check
>  my understanding I'm using the cake data set from Table 7.5 of Cochran
>  & Cox.  .....
   .
   .
   .
>                    ..... I can't figure out what error term I need to
>  specify to match C&C's results. Surely I'm missing something simple,
>  but I've been staring at this for two hours, and I haven't been able
>  to figure out the answer yet.

I don't think that Prof. Holsinger is ``missing something simple'' at
all.  Not to put too fine a point on it, and speaking as an
absolutely committed devotee of Splus, I think that the Splus syntax
for analyzing experimental designs containing random effects
absolutely SUCKS!!!

I consider myself to be moderately competent at analyzing such
designs.  (Others may well wish to disagree with my self-assessment
of competence; I won't argue too strongly :-) .) Using Minitab or SAS
I find the analysis of such designs to be straightforward; the aov()
function in Splus flummoxes me completely.

E.g. in Minitab I analyzed the C&C cake data (and got my experimental
design students of last winter to analyze it), with the following
results:

MTB > ANOVA 'br.angle' = recipe | temp + replic + replic*recipe;
SUBC>   Random 'replic'.

Source         DF         SS         MS       F      P
recipe          2     135.09      67.54    1.58  0.224
temp            5    2100.30     420.06   20.52  0.000
recipe*temp    10     205.98      20.60    1.01  0.439
replic         14   10204.24     728.87   17.03  0.000
recipe*replic  28    1198.47      42.80    2.09  0.002
Error         210    4298.89      20.47
Total         269   18142.97

Modulo a few decimal places and some ordering and notational
differences this matches up nicely with the results in C&C.  Like
Prof. Holsinger I can't for the life of me figure out how to
reproduce the C&C analysis using aov().

When Bill Venables or someone like him tells us all how it should
be done, I ***may*** say ``Oh, duhhhhhh!!!'' and slap myself on the
forehead --- but I doubt it!

Incidentally I am firmly of the opinion that talking about ``split
plots'' as such is counter-productive, confusing, and antiquated.
The ***right*** way to think about such problems is simply to decided
which effects are random and which are fixed.  Then think about
expected mean squares, and choose the denominator of your F-statistic
so that under H_0 the ratio of the expected mean squares is 1.
Nice, neat, simple, precise, foolproof, and no super-human intuition
or visualization abilities required.

Can't some clever person write a linear modelling function for
Splus which mimics the behaviour of SAS's ``PROC GLM'' and
Minitab's GLM command (or Minitab's ANOVA command for balanced data)
and doesn't mess people's minds with ``strata'' and ``Error()'' and
other such blithering confusions?

The only ***real*** problem, as I see it, would be what to call such
a function --- since ``glm()'' is already taken by ***generalized***
linear models :-) .

                         cheers,

                              Rolf Turner
                              rolf at math.unb.ca

P. S. If anyone wants to experiment with the C&C cake data, it's
on my web page:

     http://www.math.unb.ca/~rolf/

then click on Current courses --> Stat 3373 --> Chocolate cake data.
