There are 2 lines in that output that make me really nervous, the replic
line with 14 df and the recipe*replic line with 28 df.

Thinking through the problem description I assumed that the replicates
were nested within recipe, i.e. that replicate was completely different
for each recipe.  In fact I cannot think of any way that replicate (batch)
could not be nested in recipe.  Could one single batch really have all 3
recipes applied to it?

So my first thought was that there are a total of 45 replicates (15*3)
nested in 3 recipies so we should have a single term with 42 degrees of
freedom.  The 2 lines above indicate a crossing of a total of 15
replicates (batches) and recipe.  (I'm not positive on this, my Minitab is
a bit rusty).

Dusting off my Minitab knowledge (and looking through the help files), I
get the following command as what seems to me to be the appropriate
analysis (using my assumptions above):

MTB > ANOVA 'br.angle' = recipe | replic(recipe) | temp -
temp*replic(recipe);
SUBC> random replic.

Analysis of Variance (Balanced Designs)

...

Analysis of Variance for br.angle

Source            DF         SS         MS       F      P
recipe             2     135.09      67.54    0.25  0.781
replic(recipe)    42   11402.71     271.49   13.26  0.000
temp               5    2100.30     420.06   20.52  0.000
recipe*temp       10     205.98      20.60    1.01  0.439
Error            210    4298.89      20.47
Total            269   18142.97 

which matches with what I expect, but has some differences from above.

> Modulo a few decimal places and some ordering and notational
