I don't have the book, so I can't comment on matching to what they did.

I did download your copy of the dataset (thanks) and read it into S-PLUS
then had to do some modifications, I realized that S-PLUS sees things a
little differently in the data and it assumes that all replicate 1's are
the same replicate instead of the replicate 1's within recipe 1 being
different from the replicate 1's within recipe 2, etc.

so here are my commands to read in the data, fix it so that S-PLUS likes
it, and do the analysis:

> ccake <- read.table("c:/temp/ccake.dat",header=T)
> ccake$temp <- ordered(ccake$temp)
> ccake$recipe <- factor(ccake$recipe)
> ccake$replic <- factor(paste(ccake$recipe,ccake$replic,sep="r"))
> cake.aov <- aov(br.angle ~ temp*recipe + Error(replic),
+ data=ccake)
> summary(cake.aov)
Error: replic 
          Df Sum of Sq  Mean Sq   F Value     Pr(F) 
   recipe  2    135.09  67.5444 0.2487888 0.7808856
Residuals 42  11402.71 271.4931                    

Error: Within 
             Df Sum of Sq  Mean Sq  F Value     Pr(F) 
       temp   5  2100.300 420.0600 20.51986 0.0000000
temp:recipe  10   205.978  20.5978  1.00620 0.4392694
  Residuals 210  4298.889  20.4709                   

(combining the recipe and replicate number together for the new replic
column is essential)

The aov command is equivalent to the split-plot example in the help file
for aov, just replace variable names.  And other than some rounding, it
matches with the Minitab output (mine not the original version).

The possibly tricky part is to recode the replic column so that it is
clear to S-PLUS that there are 45 batches not just 15.

> When Bill Venables or someone like him tells us all how it should
> be done, I ***may*** say ``Oh, duhhhhhh!!!'' and slap myself on the
> forehead --- but I doubt it!

Well I'm not Bill Venables, and you still may not agree with my analysis,
but S-PLUS does give the same output as Minitab for equivalent problems.

...

Hope this helps,

