From J.Badcock at exeter.ac.uk  Wed Jun  7 14:58:26 2000
From: J.Badcock at exeter.ac.uk (Julie Badcock)
Date: Wed, 07 Jun 2000 15:58:26 +0100
Subject: [S] Re: Eigenvalue Problem
Message-ID: <EXECMAIL.1000607155826.F@msorpc13.ex.ac.uk>

Dear all, 

thanks to all those of you who took the time to respond to my problem 
of S-Plus 3.3 not necessarily calculating eigenvalues in descending 
order. In particular, thank you Sam Buttrey and Rolf Turner who sent 
the following answers as I now have properly descending eigenvalues 
without using a sorting algorithm!

Cheers,
        Julie.

Sam:

There's something tricky going on here. S-Plus has classes called 
"matrix,"
with a lower-case "m," and "Matrix," with an upper-case "M," and they're
different. It looks as if your "MatrixA" is a "Matrix." eigen() is a 
generic
function: when you call it with a "Matrix" argument, you're really 
calling
eigen.Matrix() rather than eigen.default(), which gets called if your
argument is a "matrix."

Check out the help for eigen.Matrix (after you issue your "library"
command). It's not clear how it sorts the eigenvalues, although it 
surely
looks as if they're ascending. Now try this:

eigen (as.matrix (MatrixA))

That will call eigen.default and give you the values in descending 
order.

I hope this helps,

Sam Buttrey
buttrey at nps.navy.mil

Rolf:

The lack of decreasing order may be due to the fact that you
are invoking the ``matrix'' library and ***not*** using the vanilla
Splus eigen() function.  When I used eigen() on your matrix I got

[1] 0.5075873 0.5075850 0.5075480 0.5067850 0.5042291 0.4650318 
0.4515315
[8] 0.1619356

(in Splus 3.4 under Solaris 2.7).  Descending order as claimed by the
doc'n.

If you indeed need to use the matrix library, and if the eigen()
function in that library is indeed returning eigenvalues in an
inconvenient order, then you can indeed use rev(sort(...)), as you
point out, to rectify the problem.  (You may want to sort the eigen
vectors into the same order as well, depending on what you're doing,
which would require you to create a ``sorting index'' using order(),
rather than using sort().)

                                        cheers,

                                                Rolf Turner
                                                rolf at math.unb.ca

----------------------
Julie Badcock
University of Exeter

