Subject: Re: [S] generating C/C++ (scoring) code from model objects
References: <75F7304BB41CD411B06600A0C98414FC0C79B7@ORSMSX54>
Message-ID: <393FB240.9A693EAF@virginia.edu>

Colin,

The Design library has 3 functions related to your question, but
only for the case where the model is a regression model (ols,
binary or ordinal logistic, Cox or parametric survival, etc.).

- latex(fit) will create LaTeX code expressing the fit algebraically
- Function(fit) will create an S function containing the algebraic form
   of the fit as long as third-order interactions are not present and you
   use one of Design's spline or polynomial functions when you use
   splines or ordinary polynomials
- sascode(Function(fit)) translates the regression equation to SAS
   syntax

It would not be hard to create a Ccode function using these tools, at
least for regression models.

See the bottom of P. 177 in
http://hesweb1.med.virginia.edu/biostat/s/doc/splus.pdf
for an example of latex().

See http://hesweb1.med.virginia.edu/biostat/s/help/Function.html
for documention of the Function function in Design.

As an aside you might want to look at the literature comparing recursive
partitioning with regression models in terms of predictive discrimination.
Also, take a good look at rpart() (see StatLib) as a substitute for tree().

-Frank Harrell

