From korourke at lri.ca  Thu Jun  8 14:56:23 2000
From: korourke at lri.ca (O'Rourke, Keith)
Date: Thu, 08 Jun 2000 10:56:23 -0400
Subject: Re: [S] Interpreting stepwise results in Poisson regression
Message-ID: <1F6254B50D8FD211BC8900E0291B03B301B67428@EXCHANG1>

>>       I am trying to detect changes in trends of deaths due
>> to AIDS-defining opportunistic infections, like this:
> >
cut
>>
> >      How can I justify leaving these variables in the
> >equation to my M.D. colleague ?

>AIC and stepwise fitting have different aims.  AIC is trying to pick a
>model that will *predict* well, and so errs on the side of including 
>marginal variables.  Sequential chisq or F tests are trying to select a
>model all of whose terms are justified, that is a good *explanation*.

>Brian D. Ripley,                  ripley at stats.ox.ac.uk

EXPLANATION was starred but perhaps could been more boldly put as 
"description under the (unrealistic?) assumption that variation is (best
thought of as) random"

although subject to some interpretation by me, I think consideration of
confounder selection 
or Rubin's propensity score approach (modeling exsposure rather than
outcomes) would be 
more helpful in these "observational data" situations.

A somewhat "tongue in cheek" account of these issues along with possibly
useful references 
is available at the bottom of
http://www.health.ucalgary.ca/~littlep/e_may99.html

Some additional references

S Greenland, JM Robins, and J Pearl. Confounding and callapsibility in
causal inference. Statistical Science 14 (1):29-46, 1999.

DB Rubin. Practical implications of modes of statistical inference for
causal effects and the critical role of the assignment mechanism. Biometrics
47:1213-1234, 1991.

Hope this may be of interest to some.

Keith O'Rourke
Statistician, Department of Surgery and Clinical Epidemiology Unit
University of Ottawa/ Ottawa Hospital

