Subject: RE: [S] Interpreting stepwise results in Poisson regression
In-Reply-To: <1F6254B50D8FD211BC8900E0291B03B301B67428@EXCHANG1>
References: <1F6254B50D8FD211BC8900E0291B03B301B67428@EXCHANG1>
Message-ID: <001b01bfd15f$78fd0790$c10616ac@oehl.bbdo.de>

> although subject to some interpretation by me, I think
> consideration of
> confounder selection
> or Rubin's propensity score approach (modeling exsposure rather than
> outcomes) would be
> more helpful in these "observational data" situations.

to my understanding:

Using the propensity score as a correction of confounding will do as well as
correcting for the propensity score predictors IF a certain linearity
assumption is met. Furthermore, using propensity score correction, one at
best ends up with a model of the average effect of the 'treatment'; if the
treatment effect depends on the propensity score predictors, including the
propensity score predictors into the model might be more approbriate than
including the propensity score.

Whether or not a variable should be corrected for (directly or via
propensity scores), depends on contextual information, and no automatic
method can decide that for us, unfortunately ...

Any comments?

Jens Oehlschlägel-Akiyoshi

