From canty at cicma.concordia.ca  Fri Jun  9 15:06:04 2000
From: canty at cicma.concordia.ca (Angelo Canty)
Date: Fri, 09 Jun 2000 11:06:04 -0400
Subject: Re: [S] S+ functions to test for multimodality
References: <Pine.OSF.4.21.0006061432040.466917-100000@snow.mtholyoke.edu>
Message-ID: <394107DC.AD84A5ED@discrete.concordia.ca>

Hi Aaron,

Your code seems like overkill for a simple problem like this.  For
a much simpler set of code see Practical 5 in Davison and Hinkley (1997)
which uses my boot library available from Statlib.  

Some comments and criticisms.
1.  You are not finding the asl at all.  You are finding the average
    difference between the number of modes in the bootstrap samples
    and the original number of modes (nm).  The correct asl is
    sum(modeslist > omode)/(nboot+1)

2.  Your function critmode does a terrible job of finding the  critical
    bandwidth.  It is a very inefficient way to do a search and will
    only return a critical value which is one of the 99 values you
specify.
    For example (using paulsen in library(boot))
    > critmode(paulsen$y,1)
    [1] 1.992308
    > nummodes(paulsen$y,1.86)
    [1] 2
    > nummodes(paulsen$y, 1.87)
    [1] 1
    so the correct value is 1.87.  You need a much better method of
    searching such as a simple binary search.

3.  I don't understand you function smoothboot at all.  You only
    generate one set of n error variates and use this for all of
    the bootstrap samples so they are no longer independent.  I
    don't have Efron and Tibshirani in front of me but I rather doubt
    this is what they suggest.  In any case sampling from a kernel
    density estimate is just a convolution of normal densities so
    all you need to do is
    xstar <- ystar +  hk * matrix(rnorm(length(ystar)),ncol=lendata
