From rjk_walter at hotmail.com  Wed Jun 14 06:17:38 2000
From: rjk_walter at hotmail.com (Ronald Walter)
Date: Wed, 14 Jun 2000 06:17:38 +0000
Subject: [S] Summary Getting (X'X)^-1. and how to calculatie the Wald statistic
Message-ID: <20000614061738.34031.qmail@hotmail.com>

Thanks to all who responded!

A list of answers is below my next question:

I wanted the (X'X)^-1 to calculate the so called Wald statistic. In a linear 
regression model y=Xb+e you can test a set of linear restrictions:

H0: Rb=q

You can base a test of H0 on the Wald statistic defined by:

W=d'(Var(d)^-1)d

where d=Rb-q
and Var(d)=s^2R((X'X)^-1)R'

Is there (numerically) a better way than just simply calculating all the 
matrices and vectors in S-PLUS and multiply them to get W?

Thanks,
Ronald Walter

_________________________________________________________

Original question:

When I fit linear models of the form y=Xb+e with lm, I like to get the  
matrix (X'X)^-1.

Right now, I am using the construction:
out <- lm(y~.,data=testdata, x=T)
solve(t(out$x)%*%out$x)

S-PLUS must be calculating (X'X)^1 internally to estimate b, would it be 
possible to get (X'X)^-1, saving me a matrix multiplication and an inverse 
operation.

Joseph F.  Lucke:

The easiest way to get (X'X)^{-1} from lm is to access the R matrix from the

QR decomposition, which is (in your setup) out$R.  Setting 
Rinv<-solve(out$R),

you have

(X'X)^{-1} = Rinv %*% t(Rinv).

Brian D. Ripley:
It isn't. It uses a QR decomposition.  But use summary(out)$cov.unscaled
to get (X'X)^{-1} a better way.

Dr. James H. Roger:

It is very unlikely that it calcuates (X'X)^-1 as it will use some sort of 
sweep operation to solve the simultaneous equation problem. This is more 
efficient.

Dr. Luciano Molinari:
S+ does not usually calculate (X'X)^-1 internally, that calculation is
numerically not recommended, and one wonders, why exactly you need (X'X)^-1.
If really you do, you can get it by calling explicitly the qr function.

