Subject: [S] Confidence ellipses on canonical variate plots (long)
Message-ID: <4.3.2.7.2.20000614071114.00b4b5e0@127.0.0.1>

I asked this question about a year ago but did not get an answer 
then.  Since then, I've tried a variety of approaches, none 
successfully.  So, let me try again.

The program SYSTAT has a very useful (to me anyway) way of displaying any 
set of grouped bivariate data.  I find it especially useful when 
visualizing two-dimensional scatterplots of canonical variate data from 
multiple groups.  The genesis of this type of plot comes from 
Gnanadesikan's "Methods for Statistical Data Analysis of Multivariate 
Observations" (first edition).

The SYSTAT routine:

1) takes the standard output from a canonical variates (or multiple 
discriminant) analysis and plots CV1 vs CV2 using different symbols for the 
cases in each group.  It also uses different colors for the symbols at the 
same time.  (This is straightforward enough to do in S-Plus; VR2 & VR3 have 
a nice example of doing this).

2.  It then superimposes bivariate confidence ellipses on each of the 
groups, centered on each group's mean and oriented so that the major axis 
is in the direction of the correlation between the values of the x and y 
variates within each group.  The ellipses are also scaled in relation to 
the sample size of each group.  The size of the confidence elllipse (e.g. 
90%, 95%, 68%) can be controlled either in syntax (S-Plus command line 
equivalent) or via a menu box using the SYSTAT GUI.

There have been numerous postings here regarding confidence ellipses, and 
I've carefully saved each of them hoping to find the one germ of 
information that would permit me to display the results of the S-Plus 
discrim() function or VR's lda() function in a comparable way.

As I say, step 1 is no problem and ample examples exist for doing it.

There is also a nice example of plotting a bivariate confidence ellipse in 
Selvin's "Modern Applied Biostatistical Methods Using S-Plus", 1998, Oxford 
University Press (pp. 102 - 103); however Selvin's example plots confidence 
ellipse(s) rather than a single ellipse corresponding to a desired 
confidence limit.

The limiting factor (for me) seems to be figuring out a way to combine the 
information needed to (1) use different plotting symbols for each group and 
(2) use different colors for each group and (3) place individual confidence 
ellipses around each group's centroid.

I'm sure I must be missing something obvious here and it may be easier to 
continue doing what I've been doing - running the analysis in S-Plus and 
then exporting the results to Systat, where this display paradigm is 
trivially easy.  On the other hand, I *know* that this should be doable in 
S-Plus and I'm sufficiently challenged by it to want to know how.  (At the 
same time, the relatively sparse response to questions about ellipses 
suggests that either this *isn't* as easy as it ought to be, that it is a 
FAQ with an answer somewhere other than the usual sources -- I've looked, 
or that it isn't sufficiently interesting, challenging or important enough 
that more than a few of us care.

If anyone has successfully accomplished this in S-Plus, would you care to 
share either code or hints on how to accomplish it.  I've tried using 
contour and countourplot.  I've used panel functions.  The best I've been 
able to come up with is a nice scatterplot with different plot symbols and 
colors for each group, and a single ellipse superimposed over the entire 
multivariate sample, not one ellipse centered over each sample.

Thanks.

