From Mike.Prager at noaa.gov  Wed Jun 14 16:54:15 2000
From: Mike.Prager at noaa.gov (Mike Prager)
Date: Wed, 14 Jun 2000 12:54:15 -0400
Subject: [S] Error messages
Message-ID: <4.3.2.7.2.20000614122427.00af2b70@nsmail.ccfhrb.noaa.gov>

(S-Plus 2000 [5 Nov 99] on Windows NT 4.0 + SP6)

This is primarily an enhancement request to MathSoft, but at the risk of 
exposing my own mistakes, I thought I'd invite comment from the list.

Some background:  After using S-Plus for a year, I am finally beginning to 
be comfortable with it, and indeed using SAS (which I know quite well) can 
seem like putting on a straitjacket.  However, one recent quirk in S-Plus 
sent me to SAS, gratefully.

I refer to useless error messages generated by S-Plus when reading an ASCII 
file with a bad record.  The particular defective record resulted in an 
unclosed quoted string, and the S-Plus error message (from read.table) was 
something like "Newline not allowed in character variable."  That's fine, 
but it didn't mention the LINE NUMBER in which the error occurred.  When I 
tried to import the same file using the GUI, rather than commands, S-Plus 
crashed with no informative error message at all -- just a visit from Dr. 
Watson.

SAS, in contrast, immediately identified which line of the input file 
contained the error.  (I haven't see SAS crash for many years -- certainly 
not from a routine input error.)

To me, a hallmark of mature software is good error handling.  Even the 
software I write and distribute to other scientists for free counts the 
lines of input data and tells the user where an error occurred.  I suggest 
that S-Plus behavior in this case is unacceptable for software used on 
large, or even mid-sized, data sets.  (For small data sets, it's simple 
enough to look at the offending file with a text editor.)

Is S-Plus well tested with simulated user errors?  My impression is that 
its error messages are often cryptic and less than perfectly useful for 
diagnosing mistakes.  We all make mistakes.  Good software should inform us 
of them, not punish us for our imperfections.  Let's leave punishment to 
the penal system, and have software serve us, not the other way around!

--
